# zfinder - A Fuzzy File Finder

A simple fuzzy finder for searching files and directories in a base directory recursively.
You can search for files through their name, extension, size, type, and even permissions.
File names are matched *fuzzily*, that is, they can or cannot be an exact match of the target.

---

## How it Works

`zfinder` looks for a matching (fuzzily) file/directory, according to the provided filters, recursively in a provided base directory.
The maximum recursion depth is controllable through command-line arguments.
It shows that exact matches first (if any), and then lists the fuzzy matched results, ranked, below that.
Each result entry is color-marked to show which part of the name was matched (if name or extension filters were applied).
Each search is multi-threaded at the recursion level, meaning every sub-search gets it's own thread. This is controllable.
There is no sense of order to the entries except for the rankings.
Filters are applied in the order they appear. It means the filter that comes first is applied first.

---

## How to Use it

`zfinder` is a CLI program. It works with command-line arguments to apply filters, and recieve other configuration data.
Following are the command-line arguments that are (or will be) supported by `zfinder`:

Argument (Long) | Argument (Short) | Input Type      | Example            | Description
----------------|------------------|-----------------|--------------------|------------
 --name         | -n               | string          | `--name "log"`     | Fuzzily matches the name of the item.
 --type         | -t               | char            | `--type f`         | Exactly matches the type of the item. Supported: `f` = file, `d` = directory, `l` = symbolic links.
 --extension    | -e               | string          | `-e "txt"`         | Exactly matches the extension of the item. Can take an empty string to match items without an extension.
 --size         | -s               | size            | `--size 100M`      | Fuzzily matches the size of the item.
 --permissions  | -p               | string          | `-p rx`            | Exactly maches the permissions of the item. The input is a list: `r` = read, `w` = write, `x` = execute.
 --max-depth    | -d               | number          | `-d 2`             | Limits the maximum recursion depth.
 --no-threading | -nt              | NONE            | `-nt`              | Avoids multi-threading in the search.
 --no-color     | -nc              | NONE            | `--no-color`       | Avoid using colors in the output.
 --no-stream    | -ns              | NONE            | `-ns`              | Performs the whole search and then lists results, instead of streaming output.

### Important Notes

- Size is a number which can have a postfix attached. Size is calculated in bytes, not bits. The postfixes supported include:
  - K = times $$10^3$$
  - M = times $$10^6$$
  - G = times $$10^9$$
  - T = times $$10^12$$
  - k = times $$2^10$$
  - m = times $$2^20$$
  - g = times $$2^30$$
  - t = times $$2^40$$
- By default, exact matches are streamed as output while fuzzy matches are printed at the end.

---

## How to Install it

You can run this by simply clone this repository and compile it from source, or download a pre-built binary from releases.

### Building from Source

Clone the repository using:
```bash
git clone https://github.com/uniqoder/zfinder.git
```

Compile it:
```bash
make
```

Now you can simply use the binary in the `build` directory of the cloned repo, or install it using make so you can run it from anywhere:
```bash
make install
```

### Installing from Releases

Just go to [releases](https://github.com/uniqoder/zfinder/releases) and install the latest pre-built binary.
